  "title": "06_The OAuth 2.0 framework",
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Objective",
      "description": "### Module objectives : \n- What is OAuth 2.0?\n- The Authorization Code Flow\n- Register applications in Keycloak\n- Generate access tokens via Keycloak\n- Configure audience and client scopes in Keycloak\n- Use refresh tokens\n- Configure Postman to request access tokens"
    },
    {
      "file": "Notes/Note.md",
      "description": "# OAuth 2.0 defines grant “flows” for obtaining access tokens. \n\nThe common ones are:\n\n- Authorization Code (with PKCE for public clients): user signs in, app gets an auth code, exchanges it for tokens. Best for web, SPAs, mobile.\n- Client Credentials: machine-to-machine; no user. App uses its own credentials to get a token.\n- Device Authorization: for devices with limited input; user completes auth on another device.\n- Refresh Token: renews access tokens without re-prompting the user.\n\n### Deprecated/avoid:\n\nImplicit: replaced by Authorization Code + PKCE.\n- Resource Owner Password Credentials: only for legacy, high-trust scenarios.\nEach flow issues an access token (and sometimes refresh/ID tokens if OpenID Connect is used).\n\n<span style=\"color: #e53935;\">we are going to focus on the Authentication flow (see the screenshot)</span>\n\n",
      "line": 126
    }
  ],
  "ref": "Notes"
}